wcristalle@hotmail.com (Artemis the Ranger) wrote in message news:<a5bfc5f.0311042305.54d23843@posting.google.com>...
> Okay, two new ideas for conducts (don't know whether they're feasible
> or not, but I'll just throw them out and see what people think):
> 
> 1) Packrat Conduct
> 
> As a packrat, you are a character who just can't bear to throw
> anything away that's of any value at all. The consequence is that you
> have to clean out the *entire dungeon* and take every object ever
> created with you when you ascend -- either carrying it in your
> inventory, wearing it, or having consumed or destroyed it (i.e. by
> burning, eating, etc.)
> Exceptions to this rule would include: rocks (there's just too many of
> them), iron chains, and other (if any) objects with no monetary value;
> lodestones; gold coins (this is covered by my second suggested
> conduct); and, finally, objects that belong to shopkeepers. This means
> that you can sell objects in return for gold, but can't go on dropping
> objects in return for "credit" once the shopkeeper's store of money
> has been exhausted (otherwise, you could dump all unwanted objects
> inside a shop.) (In other words, if you have any credit when you
> ascend, you do not satisfy packrat conduct, though temporary credit
> could be allowable.)

I think someone in the deep dark past did this once... made sure every
item on every level of the dungeon was in his possession or destroyed.
 This may have been back when geletanious cubes ate a lot more things
than they do now...
 
> 2. Scrooge Conduct
> 
> Similar to the conduct above. This time you are a super-greedy
> character who just can't bear to part with any gold. As a consequence,
> when you ascend you must carry with you every piece of gold ever
> created in the dungeon (perhaps gems as well). You can't donate to
> priests (who ever heard of Scrooge giving away money?) but you can buy
> things from shops. You must kill every gold-carrying monster you find
> and take their gold with you when you ascend; this includes priests
> and shopkeepers (except Izchak, of course.) You cannot polymorph into
> a metallivore and eat the gold to get rid of it. In other words, every
> single piece of gold ever generated must be with you when you ascend.
> 
> Any comments?
> 
> Artemis the Ranger


And exactly how would you presume to code all this up?  There is a lot
of gold created in the dungeon which you have to tunnel to get to (not
to mention sometimes digging a pit).  The only way you'd know would be
to quaff a !oOD, read a ?oGD, or cast +oDetTreas on every level.

Seeing as how all the current conducts are booleans that start off
false and at some point in time may get set true, these would be much
harder to track.  Say you enter level 1 and gold is created.  Since
you obviously haven't picked it up yet, the flag is toggled.  Then,
you pick up all the gold, flag toggled back.  Then, a group of hill
orcs is created, 5 of them carrying gold.  The flag is toggled once
again.  Now, you don't realize the orcs were created, and leave the
level.  You think you've got it all, but you really don't... 
impossible to obtain, even if you could come up with a decent way to
track it.

I think an interesting idea would be never used gold.  It's very
simple to track that a character never picks up any gold, thus never
buys anything from shops, never buys divine protection, etc.  If you
find something in a shop that you want, you have to have your pet
steal it for you, or do it yourself, dealing with the kops and angry
shopkeep on your own.

Getting your candles would be quite the challenge, especially if you
hold to the notion that Izchak is untouchable...  Of course, you could
teleport him out of his shop, take the goods and run for the stairs. 
I actually did this in a recent game when I was permanently invisible,
had the town guard mad at me for dipping for Excalibur in one of their
fountains, and didn't want the alignment penalty for killing
(previously) peaceful humans.  Putting on a mummy wrapping would have
led them right to me...  I preferred slipping past them unnoticed.

